export const NOTES = {
  skeletal: { title: "The skeletal system", lead: "206 bones in an adult: the **axial skeleton** (skull, vertebral column, ribs and sternum) protects the brain, cord and thoracic organs; the **appendicular skeleton** (girdles and limbs) gives leverage for movement. Clinically, bone anatomy is mostly about what lies next to it.",
    sections: [
      ["Bone structure and growth", "Long bones have a diaphysis (shaft) of compact bone around a medullary cavity, and epiphyses of spongy bone at each end. Until the end of puberty they grow in length at the **epiphyseal (growth) plate**, by endochondral ossification.", "- Periosteum: a vascular, richly innervated outer layer (why fractures hurt).", "- Osteoblasts lay down osteoid; osteoclasts resorb bone; osteocytes sense load (Wolff's law: bone remodels to the stress put on it).", "- Red marrow (haematopoiesis) persists in adults in the axial skeleton and proximal femur and humerus.", "! Salter–Harris fractures involve the growth plate in children. Types II (through the plate and metaphysis) are commonest; types III–V risk growth arrest."],
      ["Fracture healing", "Haematoma → inflammation → soft callus (fibrocartilage, 2–3 weeks) → hard callus (woven bone, 6–12 weeks) → remodelling into lamellar bone over months to years.", "- Describe a fracture: bone, site, pattern (transverse, oblique, spiral, comminuted), displacement, angulation, open or closed, neurovascular status.", "- Delayed union risk: smoking, diabetes, steroids, poor blood supply (scaphoid, femoral neck, distal tibia).", "! Open fractures need IV antibiotics within 1 hour, tetanus cover, a photo and a saline-soaked dressing, then debridement in theatre."],
      ["The skull and the cranial cavity", "The cranial fossae (anterior, middle, posterior) hold the frontal lobes, the temporal lobes and the brainstem/cerebellum. Foramina carry the cranial nerves and vessels.", "- Optic canal: CN II and ophthalmic artery. Superior orbital fissure: III, IV, V1, VI.", "- Foramen rotundum: V2. Foramen ovale: V3. Jugular foramen: IX, X, XI and the internal jugular vein.", "> Extradural haematoma: arterial (middle meningeal), biconvex (lens-shaped), with a lucid interval. Subdural: venous (bridging veins), crescent-shaped, often elderly or alcohol-dependent."],
      ["The vertebral column", "Typical vertebra: body, pedicles, laminae, spinous and transverse processes; the vertebral foramen holds the cord. Regional features help identify each level.", "- Cervical: bifid spinous processes, foramina transversaria for the vertebral arteries. C7 = vertebra prominens.", "- Thoracic: heart-shaped bodies, costal facets, long downward spines.", "- Lumbar: large kidney-shaped bodies, no costal facets or transverse foramina.", "Ligaments: anterior and posterior longitudinal ligaments, ligamentum flavum (pierced in a lumbar puncture, felt as a 'give'), interspinous and supraspinous.", "! Lumbar puncture layers: skin, subcutaneous fat, supraspinous ligament, interspinous ligament, ligamentum flavum, epidural space, dura, arachnoid, then CSF in the subarachnoid space."],
      ["The thoracic cage", "Twelve thoracic vertebrae, twelve pairs of ribs and the sternum. The thoracic inlet (T1, rib 1, manubrium) carries the trachea, oesophagus, great vessels and nerves.", "- Typical ribs (3–9) have a head with two facets, a neck, tubercle, angle and costal groove.", "- Pump-handle movement of upper ribs increases the AP diameter; bucket-handle movement of lower ribs increases the transverse diameter.", "! A cervical rib can compress the subclavian artery or lower trunk of the brachial plexus (thoracic outlet syndrome)."],
      ["Upper and lower limb", "Nerve injuries predicted by the bone they touch are core exam knowledge:", "- Surgical neck of humerus → axillary nerve. Mid-shaft humerus → radial nerve. Medial epicondyle → ulnar nerve.", "- Fibular neck → common fibular nerve (foot drop). Posterior hip dislocation → sciatic nerve.", "The hip is a deep ball-and-socket joint (stability first); the shoulder is shallow (mobility first). The knee relies on ligaments (cruciates and collaterals) and menisci.", "! Ottawa rules decide when an ankle or knee injury needs an X-ray: bony tenderness at specific points or inability to weight bear four steps."],
      ["Bone disease", "- **Osteoporosis**: low bone mass, T-score ≤ −2.5 on DEXA. Fragility fractures: hip, vertebrae, distal radius. Treat with bisphosphonates, calcium and vitamin D.", "- **Osteomalacia** (rickets in children): defective mineralisation from low vitamin D; low calcium and phosphate, high ALP.", "- **Paget's disease**: disordered remodelling, raised ALP with normal calcium; bone pain, deafness, high-output heart failure.", "- Bone metastases come mostly from breast, prostate, lung, kidney and thyroid ('BLT with a Kosher Pickle')."],
    ] },
  muscular: { title: "The muscular system", lead: "Skeletal muscle moves the skeleton under voluntary control, holds posture and makes heat. For each muscle, learn its **attachments, action, nerve supply** and the clinical test that checks it.",
    sections: [
      ["How muscle contracts", "A motor neuron releases acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction; the muscle fibre depolarises, the action potential runs down T-tubules, and the sarcoplasmic reticulum releases **Ca²⁺**.", "- Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, moving tropomyosin off the actin binding sites.", "- Myosin heads bind actin and pull (the power stroke); ATP releases them. Sliding filament theory: the A band stays the same length, the I band and H zone shorten.", "- No ATP after death → myosin stays bound → rigor mortis.", "! Myasthenia gravis: antibodies to the acetylcholine receptor cause fatigable weakness (ptosis, diplopia), worse later in the day."],
      ["Fibre types", "- **Type I** (slow oxidative): red, many mitochondria and myoglobin, fatigue-resistant: posture, endurance.", "- **Type IIa** (fast oxidative-glycolytic) and **IIx** (fast glycolytic): white, powerful, fatigue quickly: sprinting.", "Motor units are recruited small to large (Henneman's size principle)."],
      ["Head, neck and upper limb", "- Sternocleidomastoid and trapezius: spinal accessory nerve (CN XI). Test: shrug the shoulders, turn the head against resistance.", "- Rotator cuff (SITS): supraspinatus (abduction 0–15°), infraspinatus and teres minor (lateral rotation), subscapularis (medial rotation).", "- Deltoid: axillary nerve. Biceps and brachialis: musculocutaneous. Triceps: radial nerve.", "- Hand: thenar muscles mostly median (LOAF: lateral two lumbricals, opponens pollicis, abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis); everything else ulnar.", "! Supraspinatus tendinopathy gives a painful arc between 60° and 120° of abduction."],
      ["Trunk and abdominal wall", "Rectus abdominis in front; external oblique, internal oblique and transversus abdominis at the sides, whose aponeuroses form the rectus sheath and the linea alba.", "- The inguinal canal runs above the medial half of the inguinal ligament: it carries the spermatic cord (or round ligament) and the ilioinguinal nerve.", "- Indirect inguinal hernias pass through the deep ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels; direct hernias push through Hesselbach's triangle, medial to them.", "! Femoral hernias lie below and lateral to the pubic tubercle, are more common in women, and have a high risk of strangulation: refer urgently."],
      ["Lower limb", "- Hip flexors: iliopsoas (femoral nerve/L1–3). Extensors: gluteus maximus (inferior gluteal nerve). Abductors: gluteus medius and minimus (superior gluteal nerve).", "- Knee extension: quadriceps (femoral). Knee flexion: hamstrings (sciatic, tibial part).", "- Ankle dorsiflexion: tibialis anterior (deep fibular). Plantarflexion: gastrocnemius and soleus (tibial).", "! A positive Trendelenburg sign (pelvis drops on the unsupported side when standing on one leg) means weak abductors on the standing side: superior gluteal nerve or hip pathology."],
      ["Myotomes and reflexes", "- Shoulder abduction C5, elbow flexion C6, elbow extension C7, finger flexion C8, finger abduction T1.", "- Hip flexion L2, knee extension L3, ankle dorsiflexion L4, big toe extension L5, plantarflexion S1.", "- Reflexes: biceps C5–6, supinator C6, triceps C7, knee L3–4, ankle S1.", "> Upper motor neurone lesions: increased tone, brisk reflexes, upgoing plantars, no wasting. Lower motor neurone: wasting, fasciculation, reduced tone and reflexes."],
      ["Muscle damage", "- **Rhabdomyolysis**: muscle breakdown releases myoglobin and potassium; very high CK, dark urine, AKI risk. Treat with aggressive IV fluids.", "- **Compartment syndrome**: pressure in a closed fascial compartment exceeds perfusion. Pain out of proportion and on passive stretch; pulses are lost late. Treat with fasciotomy.", "- Statins can cause myalgia and, rarely, myositis or rhabdomyolysis."],
    ] },
  nervous: { title: "The nervous system", lead: "The **central nervous system** (brain and spinal cord) integrates; the **peripheral nervous system** (12 cranial and 31 pairs of spinal nerves) connects it to the body. Localising a lesion means asking: which tract, which side, which level?",
    sections: [
      ["Neurons and signalling", "Resting membrane potential is about −70 mV (Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, K⁺ leak). An action potential opens voltage-gated Na⁺ channels (depolarisation), then K⁺ channels (repolarisation).", "- Myelin (Schwann cells peripherally, oligodendrocytes centrally) allows **saltatory conduction** between nodes of Ranvier.", "- Multiple sclerosis demyelinates the CNS; Guillain–Barré the PNS (ascending weakness after an infection).", "> Local anaesthetics block voltage-gated Na⁺ channels; small, unmyelinated pain fibres (C fibres) are blocked first."],
      ["The brain", "- **Frontal lobe**: motor cortex, Broca's area, personality and executive function.", "- **Parietal**: somatosensory cortex; non-dominant lesions cause neglect.", "- **Temporal**: hearing, Wernicke's area, memory (hippocampus); superior quadrantanopia.", "- **Occipital**: visual cortex; homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing (PCA stroke).", "Blood supply: the circle of Willis, fed by the internal carotids and the basilar artery (from the vertebrals).", "! FAST: facial droop, arm weakness, speech problems, time to call. Thrombolysis within 4.5 h of onset if CT excludes haemorrhage; thrombectomy for large-vessel occlusion."],
      ["Ventricles and CSF", "CSF is made by the choroid plexus (~500 ml/day), flows from the lateral ventricles → third ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → fourth ventricle → subarachnoid space, and is absorbed by arachnoid granulations.", "- Hydrocephalus: obstructive (blocked flow) or communicating (poor absorption).", "- Normal pressure hydrocephalus: wet, wobbly and wacky (incontinence, gait apraxia, dementia).", "! Raised intracranial pressure: headache worse lying down and in the morning, vomiting, papilloedema; Cushing's triad (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing) is a late sign."],
      ["Spinal cord tracts", "- **Dorsal columns** (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus): fine touch, vibration, proprioception. Ipsilateral in the cord, cross in the medulla.", "- **Spinothalamic**: pain and temperature. Cross within 1–2 segments, so they're contralateral in the cord.", "- **Lateral corticospinal**: voluntary movement. Cross in the medulla, descend ipsilaterally.", "> Syringomyelia damages crossing spinothalamic fibres first: a cape-like loss of pain and temperature with preserved touch.", "! Subacute combined degeneration (B12 deficiency) hits the dorsal columns and corticospinal tracts: loss of vibration, brisk knees with absent ankle reflexes, upgoing plantars."],
      ["Cranial nerves", "I olfactory · II optic · III oculomotor · IV trochlear · V trigeminal · VI abducens · VII facial · VIII vestibulocochlear · IX glossopharyngeal · X vagus · XI accessory · XII hypoglossal.", "- III palsy: eye 'down and out', ptosis, dilated pupil (a surgical third from a posterior communicating aneurysm affects the pupil first).", "- VI palsy: can't abduct; a false localising sign of raised ICP.", "- VII: an UMN lesion spares the forehead (bilateral cortical supply); Bell's palsy (LMN) does not.", "- XII: tongue deviates **towards** the side of the lesion."],
      ["Peripheral nerves and plexuses", "Brachial plexus (C5–T1): Real Teenagers Drink Cold Beer (roots, trunks, divisions, cords, branches). Lumbosacral plexus (L1–S4): femoral, obturator, sciatic, pudendal.", "- Median: carpal tunnel, 'ape hand', loss of thumb opposition.", "- Ulnar: claw hand (4th and 5th fingers), Froment's sign.", "- Radial: wrist drop (Saturday night palsy).", "- Common fibular: foot drop, numb dorsum of foot.", "! Dermatomes worth knowing: C6 thumb, C7 middle finger, C8 little finger, T4 nipple, T10 umbilicus, L1 groin, L4 medial leg, L5 dorsum of foot, S1 lateral foot and sole."],
      ["The autonomic nervous system", "- **Sympathetic**: thoracolumbar outflow (T1–L2); short preganglionic fibres; noradrenaline at most targets. Fight or flight.", "- **Parasympathetic**: craniosacral (III, VII, IX, X and S2–4); long preganglionic fibres; acetylcholine. Rest and digest.", "> Horner's syndrome (interrupted sympathetic chain): ptosis, miosis and anhidrosis on the same side. Causes include a Pancoast tumour, carotid dissection and lateral medullary stroke."],
    ] },
  cardiovascular: { title: "The cardiovascular system", lead: "A pump, a high-pressure distribution network and a low-pressure return. Exam questions sit at the junction of anatomy and physiology: which artery supplies what, what the ECG shows, and what happens when flow fails.",
    sections: [
      ["The heart's anatomy", "The heart lies in the middle mediastinum, rotated so the **right ventricle forms most of the anterior surface** and the left atrium the posterior (so an enlarged left atrium can compress the oesophagus).", "- Borders on CXR: right border = right atrium; left border = left ventricle (and auricle); inferior = right ventricle.", "- Valves: tricuspid and mitral (atrioventricular, with chordae tendineae and papillary muscles); pulmonary and aortic (semilunar).", "- Auscultation: aortic (2nd ICS right), pulmonary (2nd ICS left), tricuspid (4th ICS left sternal edge), mitral (5th ICS mid-clavicular).", "! Cardiac tamponade (Beck's triad): hypotension, raised JVP and muffled heart sounds. Treat with pericardiocentesis."],
      ["The cardiac cycle", "Atrial systole tops up the ventricles → isovolumetric contraction (AV valves shut: S1) → ejection → isovolumetric relaxation (semilunar valves shut: S2) → passive filling.", "- Stroke volume ≈ 70 ml; ejection fraction normally >55%.", "- **Frank–Starling**: more stretch (preload) → stronger contraction, up to a point.", "- Mean arterial pressure = cardiac output × total peripheral resistance ≈ DBP + ⅓(SBP − DBP).", "> Systolic murmurs: aortic stenosis (ejection, radiates to carotids), mitral regurgitation (pansystolic, radiates to axilla). Diastolic: aortic regurgitation (early, left sternal edge), mitral stenosis (mid-diastolic rumble at apex)."],
      ["Conduction and the ECG", "SA node (60–100/min) → atria (P wave) → AV node (PR interval 120–200 ms) → His–Purkinje (QRS <120 ms) → repolarisation (T wave).", "- First-degree block: long PR. Mobitz I: PR lengthens until a beat drops. Mobitz II: sudden dropped beats (risk of complete block). Third-degree: P and QRS independent.", "- Leads: inferior II, III, aVF (RCA); anterior V1–V4 (LAD); lateral I, aVL, V5–V6 (circumflex).", "! STEMI: ST elevation ≥1 mm in two contiguous limb leads (≥2 mm chest leads) or new LBBB with symptoms → primary PCI within 120 minutes of diagnosis."],
      ["Coronary circulation", "Left main → LAD (anterior wall, septum, apex) and circumflex (lateral and posterior wall). RCA → right ventricle, inferior wall, SA node (60%) and AV node (80–90%).", "- Venous drainage: mostly into the coronary sinus, which opens into the right atrium.", "- Atherosclerosis: endothelial injury → LDL in the intima → foam cells → fatty streak → fibrous plaque. Rupture → thrombosis → acute coronary syndrome.", "> ACS management basics: aspirin 300 mg, a second antiplatelet, anticoagulation, oxygen only if SpO₂ <94%, nitrates and analgesia, then revascularisation."],
      ["The great vessels and arteries", "- Aortic arch branches: brachiocephalic (→ right subclavian and right common carotid), left common carotid, left subclavian.", "- Abdominal aorta: coeliac trunk (foregut), SMA (midgut), IMA (hindgut), renal, gonadal, lumbar arteries; bifurcates at L4.", "- Pulses to know: radial, brachial, carotid, femoral, popliteal, posterior tibial, dorsalis pedis.", "! Aortic dissection: tearing chest pain radiating to the back, unequal arm BPs, widened mediastinum. Type A (ascending) needs surgery; type B is often managed medically."],
      ["Veins and venous return", "Veins hold about 65% of blood volume. Return relies on valves, the skeletal muscle pump and the respiratory pump (negative intrathoracic pressure on inspiration).", "- Great saphenous vein: anterior to the medial malleolus → medial leg → joins the femoral vein at the saphenofemoral junction (used for CABG grafts).", "- **Virchow's triad** for thrombosis: stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulability.", "! Suspected DVT: Wells score → D-dimer (if unlikely) or proximal leg ultrasound (if likely). PE: CTPA; treat with a DOAC such as apixaban."],
      ["Blood pressure control", "- Short term: baroreceptors (carotid sinus via IX, aortic arch via X) adjust heart rate and vascular tone.", "- Long term: the kidney and **renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system**. Renin (from JG cells) → angiotensin I → ACE (lungs) → angiotensin II: vasoconstriction, aldosterone, ADH, thirst.", "- ANP from stretched atria promotes sodium excretion.", "> Hypertension treatment (NICE): under 55 and not of Black African/Caribbean origin, start an ACE inhibitor or ARB; otherwise a calcium-channel blocker. Then combine, then add a thiazide-like diuretic."],
    ] },
  respiratory: { title: "The respiratory system", lead: "Air is conducted, warmed and filtered down to the respiratory bronchioles, then exchanged across 70 m² of alveolar membrane. Ventilation, perfusion and their matching decide the blood gases.",
    sections: [
      ["Upper airway and larynx", "Nose → nasopharynx → oropharynx → laryngopharynx → larynx. The larynx protects the airway (epiglottis, cough) and makes voice.", "- Cartilages: thyroid, cricoid (the only complete ring), arytenoids, epiglottis.", "- Nerves: recurrent laryngeal (all intrinsic muscles except cricothyroid; sensation below the cords) and superior laryngeal (cricothyroid; sensation above the cords).", "! Stridor (inspiratory) suggests upper airway obstruction: epiglottitis, croup, foreign body, anaphylaxis. Don't examine the throat in suspected epiglottitis."],
      ["The bronchial tree", "Trachea → main bronchi → lobar → segmental (bronchopulmonary segments) → bronchioles → terminal bronchioles (end of the conducting zone) → respiratory bronchioles → alveolar ducts → alveoli.", "- Cartilage disappears at the bronchioles; smooth muscle remains (the site of bronchospasm in asthma).", "- Anatomical dead space ≈ 150 ml.", "> Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells makes the mucociliary escalator; smoking and cystic fibrosis impair it."],
      ["The lungs and pleura", "- Right lung: 3 lobes, 2 fissures, 10 segments. Left lung: 2 lobes, 1 fissure, cardiac notch and lingula.", "- Pleura: visceral (no pain fibres) and parietal (intercostal and phrenic nerves: sharp, pleuritic pain).", "- Surface markings: pleura reaches rib 8 midclavicular, 10 midaxillary, 12 posteriorly; the lungs two ribs higher (6, 8, 10).", "! Tension pneumothorax: respiratory distress, tracheal deviation away, hyper-resonance, absent breath sounds, hypotension. Decompress immediately (2nd ICS midclavicular or 4th/5th ICS mid-axillary), then a chest drain."],
      ["Mechanics of breathing", "Inspiration is active: the diaphragm descends and external intercostals raise the ribs, so intrathoracic pressure falls and air flows in. Quiet expiration is passive elastic recoil.", "- Compliance falls in fibrosis (stiff lungs) and rises in emphysema (floppy lungs, poor recoil).", "- Surfactant lowers surface tension (Laplace's law, P = 2T/r), stopping small alveoli collapsing.", "- Spirometry: obstructive (FEV1/FVC <0.7: asthma, COPD) vs restrictive (ratio normal or high, FVC low: fibrosis, neuromuscular, chest wall)."],
      ["Gas exchange and transport", "Diffusion follows Fick's law: proportional to area and pressure gradient, inversely proportional to thickness.", "- Oxygen: 98% bound to haemoglobin. The dissociation curve shifts **right** (easier unloading) with raised CO₂, acidity, temperature and 2,3-BPG.", "- CO₂: mostly as bicarbonate (carbonic anhydrase in red cells), some carbamino, a little dissolved.", "- V/Q mismatch: shunt (perfusion without ventilation, e.g. pneumonia) vs dead space (ventilation without perfusion, e.g. PE).", "> ABG: type 1 respiratory failure = low O₂, normal/low CO₂. Type 2 = low O₂, high CO₂ (COPD, exhaustion, neuromuscular)."],
      ["Control of breathing", "Medullary centres set the rhythm. **Central chemoreceptors** respond to CSF pH (CO₂); peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid and aortic bodies) respond mainly to low O₂.", "! In some patients with COPD and chronic CO₂ retention, high-flow oxygen can worsen hypercapnia: target SpO₂ 88–92% until a blood gas guides you."],
      ["Common conditions", "- **Asthma**: reversible obstruction, eosinophilic inflammation. Life-threatening signs: PEF <33%, SpO₂ <92%, silent chest, exhaustion, normal or rising CO₂.", "- **COPD**: irreversible obstruction from smoking; emphysema and chronic bronchitis.", "- **Pneumonia**: CURB-65 (confusion, urea >7, RR ≥30, BP <90/≤60, age ≥65) guides severity.", "- **Lung cancer**: non-small cell (adenocarcinoma, squamous, large cell) and small cell (paraneoplastic SIADH, ectopic ACTH, Lambert–Eaton)."],
    ] },
  digestive: { title: "The digestive system", lead: "A 9-metre tube with glands attached. The embryological divisions (**foregut, midgut, hindgut**) predict each part's blood supply, nerve supply and where its pain is felt.",
    sections: [
      ["Foregut, midgut and hindgut", "- **Foregut** (oesophagus to the 2nd part of the duodenum, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, spleen*): coeliac trunk; pain in the epigastrium.", "- **Midgut** (2nd part of the duodenum to the proximal ⅔ of the transverse colon): SMA; periumbilical pain.", "- **Hindgut** (distal ⅓ of the transverse colon to the upper anal canal): IMA; suprapubic pain.", "*The spleen is supplied by the coeliac trunk but develops from mesoderm, not gut.", "> Visceral pain is poorly localised to the midline; once the parietal peritoneum is involved, it localises (as in appendicitis)."],
      ["Peritoneum", "Intraperitoneal organs hang on a mesentery (stomach, jejunum, ileum, transverse and sigmoid colon); retroperitoneal organs lie behind it.", "- Retroperitoneal (SAD PUCKER): suprarenals, aorta/IVC, duodenum (2nd–4th parts), pancreas (not the tail), ureters, colon (ascending and descending), kidneys, oesophagus, rectum.", "- Lesser sac is entered via the epiploic foramen (of Winslow), behind the portal triad.", "! Peritonitis: guarding, rigidity, rebound tenderness, and a still patient. A perforated viscus shows free air under the diaphragm on an erect CXR."],
      ["Oesophagus and stomach", "- Lower oesophageal sphincter: physiological, helped by the diaphragmatic crura.", "- Gastric secretion phases: cephalic (vagus), gastric (distension, gastrin), intestinal (inhibitory: secretin, CCK, GIP).", "- Acid: parietal cell H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, stimulated by gastrin, histamine (H2) and ACh. PPIs block the pump.", "! Upper GI bleed: Glasgow-Blatchford score at presentation, Rockall after endoscopy. Variceal bleeds need terlipressin and antibiotics before endoscopy."],
      ["Liver, biliary tree and pancreas", "- The liver: 8 Couinaud segments; the falciform ligament separates anatomical right and left lobes, but the functional division is Cantlie's line (gallbladder fossa to IVC).", "- Bile: made by hepatocytes, stored and concentrated in the gallbladder, released by **CCK**. Bile salts emulsify fats and are recycled through the terminal ileum (enterohepatic circulation).", "- Jaundice: pre-hepatic (haemolysis, unconjugated), hepatic, post-hepatic (obstructive: pale stools, dark urine, itch).", "> Liver function: synthetic (albumin, clotting factors: PT/INR), excretory (bilirubin), injury pattern (ALT/AST hepatitic vs ALP/GGT cholestatic)."],
      ["Small intestine: digestion and absorption", "- Carbohydrate: amylase → disaccharidases on the brush border → glucose/galactose (SGLT1, with Na⁺) and fructose (GLUT5).", "- Protein: pepsin → trypsin, chymotrypsin (activated by enterokinase) → peptidases → amino acids and small peptides.", "- Fat: lipase + bile salts → micelles → enterocytes → chylomicrons → lacteals (lymph).", "- Iron in the duodenum; B12 and bile salts in the terminal ileum.", "! Coeliac disease: anti-tTG IgA antibodies (check total IgA), villous atrophy on duodenal biopsy; the patient must still be eating gluten when tested."],
      ["Large intestine", "- Absorbs water and Na⁺, secretes K⁺ and mucus; bacteria ferment fibre to short-chain fatty acids.", "- Rectum to anal canal: the dentate (pectinate) line divides endoderm (visceral, painless, portal drainage) from ectoderm (somatic, painful, systemic drainage).", "- Haemorrhoids above the line are painless; below it, painful.", "! Colorectal cancer red flags (NICE 2WW/FIT): age ≥40 with weight loss and abdominal pain, ≥50 with rectal bleeding, ≥60 with iron-deficiency anaemia or change in bowel habit, or a rectal/abdominal mass."],
      ["Hernias and the inguinal canal", "- Inguinal canal walls (MALT from above clockwise): roof Muscles (internal oblique, transversus), Anterior wall Aponeurosis of external oblique, floor Ligament (inguinal), posterior wall Transversalis fascia (and conjoint tendon).", "- Deep ring: midpoint of the inguinal ligament. Superficial ring: above and medial to the pubic tubercle.", "> Hernia above and medial to the pubic tubercle = inguinal; below and lateral = femoral."],
    ] },
  urinary: { title: "The urinary system", lead: "The kidneys keep the internal environment constant: water, electrolytes, acid–base, waste excretion, and hormones (EPO, renin, calcitriol). The ureters, bladder and urethra carry and store the result.",
    sections: [
      ["Kidney anatomy", "- Position: T12–L3, retroperitoneal, in perirenal fat inside the renal fascia; the right is lower.", "- Cortex (glomeruli, convoluted tubules) and medulla (pyramids with loops of Henle and collecting ducts) → minor calyces → major calyces → renal pelvis.", "- Blood: renal artery → segmental → interlobar → arcuate → interlobular → afferent arteriole → glomerulus → efferent arteriole → peritubular capillaries / vasa recta.", "! The left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta under the SMA, where it can be compressed (nutcracker), and receives the left gonadal vein: a left varicocele can be a sign of a left renal tumour."],
      ["The nephron", "- **Glomerulus**: filtration through fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane and podocytes. GFR ≈ 125 ml/min.", "- **Proximal tubule**: reabsorbs ~65% of Na⁺ and water, all glucose and amino acids, most bicarbonate.", "- **Loop of Henle**: countercurrent multiplier. The thick ascending limb (NKCC2, blocked by loop diuretics) is impermeable to water.", "- **Distal tubule**: NaCl cotransporter (blocked by thiazides); calcium reabsorption under PTH.", "- **Collecting duct**: aldosterone (ENaC, K⁺ secretion) and ADH (aquaporin-2) fine-tune salt and water.", "> Glucose appears in urine when plasma glucose exceeds about 10 mmol/L (the renal threshold) or with SGLT2 inhibitors."],
      ["Acid–base", "The kidney reabsorbs filtered bicarbonate and makes new bicarbonate by excreting H⁺ as ammonium and titratable acid.", "- Metabolic acidosis: work out the anion gap = (Na⁺ + K⁺) − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻); normal ≈ 10–18.", "- Raised gap: lactate, ketones, renal failure, toxins (methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylates).", "- Normal gap: diarrhoea, renal tubular acidosis, Addison's."],
      ["Ureters, bladder and urethra", "- Ureter narrowings: PUJ, pelvic brim, VUJ (stone sites).", "- Bladder: detrusor (parasympathetic S2–4), internal sphincter (sympathetic), external sphincter (pudendal, voluntary).", "- Male urethra: prostatic, membranous, spongy. A pelvic fracture can tear the membranous urethra: blood at the meatus, so don't catheterise urethrally.", "! Urinary retention in men: most often benign prostatic hyperplasia; also constipation, anticholinergics, and cauda equina syndrome."],
      ["Kidney disease", "- **AKI**: prerenal (hypovolaemia, sepsis), renal (ATN, glomerulonephritis, interstitial nephritis), postrenal (obstruction). Stop nephrotoxins: NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, metformin, gentamicin (DAMN drugs).", "- **CKD** stages by eGFR (G1 ≥90 to G5 <15) and albuminuria (A1–A3). Complications: anaemia, renal bone disease, hyperkalaemia, acidosis, cardiovascular disease.", "- **Nephrotic syndrome**: proteinuria >3.5 g/day, hypoalbuminaemia, oedema, hyperlipidaemia. **Nephritic**: haematuria, hypertension, oliguria.", "! Hyperkalaemia with ECG changes (tall tented T waves, wide QRS): IV calcium gluconate to protect the heart, then insulin with dextrose and salbutamol to shift K⁺."],
    ] },
  endocrine: { title: "The endocrine system", lead: "Glands that signal through the blood. Most are controlled by **negative feedback** along an axis: hypothalamus → pituitary → target gland. Disease is too much, too little, or the wrong place.",
    sections: [
      ["The hypothalamic–pituitary axis", "The hypothalamus sends releasing hormones down the portal circulation to the anterior pituitary (TRH, CRH, GnRH, GHRH, and dopamine to inhibit prolactin), and neurons directly to the posterior pituitary (ADH, oxytocin).", "- Anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) derives from Rathke's pouch; posterior (neurohypophysis) from the diencephalon.", "> Testing: measure the hormone and its controller together (TSH with T4; ACTH with cortisol). Suppress to test excess, stimulate to test deficiency.", "! Pituitary apoplexy (haemorrhage into an adenoma): sudden headache, visual loss, ophthalmoplegia and adrenal crisis. Give hydrocortisone."],
      ["Thyroid", "Follicular cells trap iodide, which thyroid peroxidase attaches to thyroglobulin; T4 (mostly) and T3 (more active) are released. Most T3 comes from peripheral conversion of T4.", "- Hypothyroid: tiredness, weight gain, cold intolerance, constipation, bradycardia. Commonest cause in the UK: Hashimoto's (anti-TPO). Treat with levothyroxine.", "- Hyperthyroid: weight loss, heat intolerance, tremor, AF. Causes: Graves', toxic multinodular goitre, toxic adenoma, thyroiditis.", "- Blood supply: superior thyroid (external carotid) and inferior thyroid (thyrocervical trunk) arteries. The superior laryngeal nerve's external branch runs with the superior thyroid artery.", "! Thyroid storm: fever, tachycardia, agitation. Treat with beta-blockers, propylthiouracil, iodide (after the antithyroid drug) and steroids."],
      ["Adrenal glands", "- Cortex: glomerulosa (aldosterone, controlled by angiotensin II and K⁺), fasciculata (cortisol, controlled by ACTH), reticularis (androgens).", "- Medulla: chromaffin cells, adrenaline (80%) and noradrenaline, controlled by sympathetic preganglionic fibres.", "- **Cushing's syndrome**: excess cortisol. Commonest cause: steroid medication. Cushing's disease = pituitary ACTH adenoma. Screen with the overnight dexamethasone suppression test.", "- **Conn's syndrome**: primary hyperaldosteronism, hypertension with low K⁺. Screen with the aldosterone:renin ratio.", "! Never stop long-term steroids suddenly. Sick-day rules: double the dose with fever or illness; IM hydrocortisone if vomiting."],
      ["The endocrine pancreas", "Islets of Langerhans: β cells (insulin, ~70%), α (glucagon), δ (somatostatin).", "- Insulin: GLUT4 glucose uptake into muscle and fat, glycogen synthesis, lipogenesis; it also drives K⁺ into cells.", "- Type 1 diabetes: autoimmune β-cell destruction, insulin deficiency, ketosis risk. Type 2: insulin resistance with failing β cells.", "- Diagnosis: HbA1c ≥48 mmol/mol, fasting glucose ≥7.0, or random ≥11.1 mmol/L with symptoms.", "! DKA: glucose >11, ketones >3 mmol/L, pH <7.3 or bicarbonate <15. Treat with IV fluids, fixed-rate insulin at 0.1 units/kg/h, and potassium replacement."],
      ["Calcium and the parathyroids", "Four parathyroid glands behind the thyroid. PTH raises calcium: bone resorption, renal Ca²⁺ reabsorption, phosphate excretion and activation of vitamin D.", "- Primary hyperparathyroidism: high Ca²⁺ with high or unsuppressed PTH ('bones, stones, groans and psychic moans').", "- Hypercalcaemia of malignancy: high Ca²⁺ with suppressed PTH (PTHrP or bone metastases).", "> Hypocalcaemia signs: Chvostek's (tapping the facial nerve twitches the face) and Trousseau's (carpopedal spasm with a BP cuff)."],
    ] },
  lymphatic: { title: "The lymphatic and immune system", lead: "Lymphatic vessels return about 3 litres of filtered fluid a day to the blood, carry absorbed fat, and bring antigens to lymph nodes where the adaptive immune response begins.",
    sections: [
      ["Lymphatic vessels and drainage", "Capillaries → collecting vessels (with valves) → nodes → trunks → the **thoracic duct** (starting at the cisterna chyli, L1–2) or right lymphatic duct → the venous angles.", "- The thoracic duct passes through the aortic hiatus (T12) and ascends behind the oesophagus to the left venous angle.", "- Lymphoedema: from node removal (axillary surgery), radiotherapy, filariasis, or primary (Milroy's disease).", "> Drainage predicts spread: breast → axillary nodes (75%) and internal mammary; testis → para-aortic nodes (not inguinal); anal canal below the dentate line → inguinal nodes."],
      ["Lymphoid organs", "- Primary: bone marrow (B cells mature) and thymus (T cells mature).", "- Secondary: lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, Peyer's patches and MALT, where lymphocytes meet antigen.", "- Lymph node: cortex (B-cell follicles with germinal centres), paracortex (T cells, high endothelial venules), medulla (plasma cells).", "! Examine nodes systematically: size, consistency, tenderness, fixation, and the area they drain. Generalised lymphadenopathy suggests systemic disease (EBV, HIV, lymphoma, leukaemia, sarcoidosis)."],
      ["The spleen", "Red pulp filters blood and removes old or abnormal red cells (pitting out inclusions); white pulp holds lymphocytes around central arterioles.", "- Splenomegaly: massive (CML, myelofibrosis, malaria, leishmaniasis), moderate (portal hypertension, lymphoma, haemolytic anaemias).", "- Hyposplenism shows Howell–Jolly bodies on the blood film (sickle cell disease, coeliac disease, after splenectomy).", "! Splenic rupture after trauma or in infectious mononucleosis: avoid contact sports for at least 3–4 weeks after glandular fever."],
      ["Innate and adaptive immunity", "- **Innate**: barriers, neutrophils, macrophages, NK cells, complement, pattern-recognition receptors (TLRs). Fast, non-specific.", "- **Adaptive**: B cells (antibodies; IgM first, then class switching to IgG, IgA, IgE) and T cells (CD4 helpers via MHC II, CD8 cytotoxic via MHC I). Slow at first, specific, with memory.", "- Hypersensitivity: I (IgE, anaphylaxis), II (antibody to cell surface: Graves', haemolysis), III (immune complexes: SLE), IV (T-cell delayed: contact dermatitis, TB test).", "! Anaphylaxis: IM adrenaline 500 micrograms (0.5 ml of 1 in 1000) into the anterolateral thigh, repeat after 5 minutes if needed; then fluids and airway support."],
      ["Lymphoma and the thymus", "- Hodgkin lymphoma: Reed–Sternberg cells, bimodal age, contiguous nodal spread, pain in nodes after alcohol.", "- Non-Hodgkin lymphoma: most commonly diffuse large B-cell; more often extranodal.", "- Anterior mediastinal masses (the 4 Ts): thymoma, teratoma, thyroid (retrosternal), 'terrible' lymphoma."],
    ] },
};
